#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198004 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "e74c46c7-7f57-5adc-8acb-ea91958d8725" :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Quadratic Taylor Polynomial"}] :question/id #uuid "6c5e3d93-e244-52da-845b-365bb94bd19d" :question/math-academy-id "e-12562" :question/problem "Determine the quadratic Taylor polynomial for the function of two variables \n$f(x,y) = \\cos(x+y)$\nabout the point $(0,0).$" :question/worked-solution "If the function $f(x,y)$ is twice differentiable at $(x,y) = (0,0),$ then the quadratic Taylor polynomial of $f$ about $(0,0)$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x,y) & ≈\\overset{\\text{Linear approximation}}{\\overset{⏞}{f(0,0)+{f}_{x}(0,0)\\cdot x+{f}_{y}(0,0)\\cdot y}} \\\\  & \\,\\,+{{\\frac{1}{2}({f}_{xx}(0,0)\\cdot {x}^{2}+2{f}_{xy}(0,0)\\cdot xy+{f}_{yy}(0,0)\\cdot {y}^{2})}_{⏟}}_{\\text{Quadratic part}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we compute the terms of our Taylor polynomial.\n\n- Calculating the value of the function, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(0,0) & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Calculating the first derivatives, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{x}(x,y) & =-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x+y), & \\,{f}_{y}(x,y) & =-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x+y) \\\\ {f}_{x}(0,0) & =0, & \\,{f}_{y}(0,0) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Calculating the second derivatives, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{xx}(x,y) & =-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x\\,+\\,y), & \\,{f}_{xx}(0,0) & =-1 \\\\ {f}_{xy}(x,y) & =-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x\\,+\\,y), & \\,{f}_{xy}(0,0) & =-1 \\\\ {f}_{yy}(x,y) & =-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x\\,+\\,y), & \\,{f}_{yy}(0,0) & =-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the second-degree Taylor polynomial is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x,y) & ≈1+0\\cdot x+0\\cdot y+\\frac{1}{2}((-1)\\cdot {x}^{2}+2(-1)\\cdot xy+(-1)\\cdot {y}^{2}) \\\\  & =1-\\frac{1}{2}{x}^{2}-xy-\\frac{1}{2}{y}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198005 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "b91ae708-a63d-51ac-9ae0-f24724392858" :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Quadratic Taylor Polynomial in Matrix Form"}] :question/id #uuid "1d13a6c0-0912-5089-81ed-c298bf747ccd" :question/math-academy-id "e-12563" :question/problem "For the function $f:\\mathbb R^2\\to\\mathbb R,$ defined as\n\n$$\nf\\left(x,y \\right) \n=\nx^2y-xy^3,\n$$\n\ndetermine the quadratic Taylor polynomial of $f$ about ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14070188/assets/b799b1e1fbe7819de86c131989530033d750669ddc8fee88c711b537dad982a6.png) Give your answer in matrix form." :question/worked-solution "Suppose that the function $\\mathbf{f}:\\mathbb R^n\\to\\mathbb R$ is defined by \n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14070188/assets/b1c2d4408c64daf16466d81c1ea97bdd32f821deb858cd09bc539fc53ebf3ad8.png) \n\nand is twice differentiable at $\\mathbf x = \\mathbf a.$ Then, the quadratic Taylor polynomial of $f$ about $\\mathbf a$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & ≈f(a)+{f}^{′}(a)(x-a)+\\frac{1}{2}(x-a)^{T}{f}^{″}(a)(x-a),\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14070188/assets/c54318fa746f53209585695e2c40d80d793d2b35327b257426b4e4fa7b25463b.png)\n\nand\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14070188/assets/952dafd201f20f1da5b0ac77527beeb4f6d7c259813dbaee74a8d6796dd5d3b2.png)\n\nIn this case, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x} & \\frac{∂f}{∂y}\\end{aligned}], & \\,{f}^{″}(x) & =[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂x∂y} \\\\ \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂y∂x} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{y}^{2}}\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we compute the terms of our Taylor polynomial:\n\n- Calculating the value of the function, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(1,1) & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Calculating the first derivatives, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =[\\begin{aligned}2xy-{y}^{3} & {x}^{2}-3x{y}^{2}\\end{aligned}] \\\\ {f}^{′}(1,1) & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & -2\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Calculating the second derivative, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{″}(x) & =[\\begin{aligned}2y & 2x-3{y}^{2} \\\\ 2x-3{y}^{2} & -6xy\\end{aligned}] \\\\ {f}^{″}(1,1) & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & -1 \\\\ -1 & -6\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the second-degree Taylor polynomial (in matrix form) is given by\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14070188/assets/fc105c91cf998f6b564570697321cfb72ac30f81e88dd6c1a0decfe8495259ba.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198006 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "b19cc766-bdc5-5ab2-aa4d-ebddf8311116" :knowledge-point/title "Approximating a Multivariable Function at a Point Using a Quadratic Taylor Polynomial"}] :question/id #uuid "137d7caf-b232-5e21-9583-14d907a98624" :question/math-academy-id "e-12564" :question/problem "For the twice-differentiable function $f:\\mathbb R^3\\to\\mathbb R,$ we have the following data:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(1,0,0) & =-1, & \\,{f}^{′}(1,0,0) & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}], & \\,{f}^{″}(1,0,0) & =[\\begin{aligned}3 & 0 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 0 & 2\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUse the quadratic Taylor polynomial of $f$ about ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14070188/assets/32af7a7ab867c247eff15583688e31052e2d640b1be6c6c1b1f8bd19b7b7f2f7.png) to approximate the value of $f(1,1,1).$" :question/worked-solution "Suppose that the function $\\mathbf{f}:\\mathbb R^n\\to\\mathbb R$ is defined by \n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14070188/assets/f27bcd29776525aa543b778627e0aace4e0b0537ceac83f5750a31f5def4f762.png) \n\nand is twice differentiable at $\\mathbf x = \\mathbf a.$ Then, the quadratic Taylor polynomial of $f$ about $\\mathbf a$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & ≈f(a)+{f}^{′}(a)(x-a)+\\frac{1}{2}(x-a)^{T}{f}^{″}(a)(x-a),\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14070188/assets/c281563d780c34ea5753498e0e722bfb0296fe06c5d43e82473a45901a1aa7ee.png)\n\nand\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14070188/assets/2fa9abbaad8230a38e1085b8842fbbd970c66addff6f388e13973ee116f0cd37.png)\n\nIn this case, we have ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14070188/assets/71175744ceb56efc01b8b18b97d6681b11b8e9b85a1d899fc9a137ab5e3c611c.png) and the following data:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(a) & =-1, & \\,{f}^{′}(a) & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}], & \\,{f}^{″}(a) & =[\\begin{aligned}3 & 0 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 0 & 2\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the second-degree Taylor polynomial is given by\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14070188/assets/0d3a22cd82a02ab5122e811236d53eb4c0018e9817d2542163acc005ca5d3764.png)\n\nFinally, evaluating the polynomial at ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14070188/assets/dad24b303457c56dc14c11b3b9de6cb827b18b8f3a03059922d1b15521ea119d.png) we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(1,1,1) & ≈-1+[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1-1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]+\\frac{1}{2}{[\\begin{aligned}1-1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]}^{T}[\\begin{aligned}3 & 0 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 0 & 2\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1-1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =-1+[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]+\\frac{1}{2}{[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]}^{T}[\\begin{aligned}3 & 0 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 0 & 2\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =-1+1+\\frac{1}{2}[\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(3) \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198570 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "b91ae708-a63d-51ac-9ae0-f24724392858" :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Quadratic Taylor Polynomial in Matrix Form"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a04d5e16-2468-543d-b47f-159fc11f1a9e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3x - 1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186194322} {:answer/id #uuid "160adcd1-500b-58a4-9ea5-d6e3eb59d6fa" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2x - 3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186194323} {:answer/id #uuid "39166f28-5cf9-50de-b408-1f705693d257" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3x + 2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186194324} {:answer/id #uuid "3f14f629-91ab-5120-9d35-8dae2bc0ba57" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3x + 5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186194325} {:answer/id #uuid "7b0d156a-6959-58c1-93a8-042b4d74867f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2x + 3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186194326}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "3f14f629-91ab-5120-9d35-8dae2bc0ba57" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3x + 5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186194325} :answer-field/id #uuid "67819292-e7b9-5071-9ea9-6882b10c7f58" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186194321}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "2ec5ff3d-fd4d-5171-8304-f2a9e9fbb4f6" :question/math-academy-id "q-162989" :question/problem "For the function $f:{R}^{2}\\to R,$ defined as\n\n$$\nf(x,y)={e}^{x+2y},\n$$\n\nthe quadratic Taylor polynomial of $f$ about $a=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]$ is given by\n\n$$\nf(x)≈∗+[\\begin{aligned}1 & ∗\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}∗ \\\\ y\\end{aligned}]+\\frac{1}{2}{[\\begin{aligned}∗ \\\\ y\\end{aligned}]}^{T}[\\begin{aligned}1 & ∗ \\\\ 2 & 4\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}∗ \\\\ y\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nFind the sum of the six missing expressions." :question/worked-solution "Suppose that the function $f:{R}^{n}\\to R$ is defined by \n\n$$\nf:[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2} \\\\ ⋮ \\\\ {x}_{n}\\end{aligned}]⟶z\n$$\n\n \n\nand is twice differentiable at $x=a.$ Then, the quadratic Taylor polynomial of $f$ about $a$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & ≈f(a)+{f}^{′}(a)(x-a)+\\frac{1}{2}(x-a)^{T}{f}^{″}(a)(x-a),\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(a)={[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂{x}_{1}} & \\frac{∂f}{∂{x}_{2}} & ⋯ & \\frac{∂f}{∂{x}_{n}}\\end{aligned}]|}_{x=a}\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n{f}^{″}(a)={[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{1}^{2}} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{1}∂{x}_{2}} & ⋯ & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{1}∂{x}_{n}} \\\\ \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{2}∂{x}_{1}} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{2}^{2}} & ⋯ & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{2}∂{x}_{n}} \\\\ ⋮ & ⋮ & ⋮ & ⋮ \\\\ \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{n}∂{x}_{1}} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{n}∂{x}_{2}} & ⋯ & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{n}^{2}}\\end{aligned}]}_{x=a}\n$$\n\nIn this case, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x} & \\frac{∂f}{∂y}\\end{aligned}], & \\,{f}^{″}(x) & =[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂x∂y} \\\\ \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂y∂x} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{y}^{2}}\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we compute the terms of our Taylor polynomial:\n\n- Calculating the value of the function, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(0,0) & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Calculating the first derivatives, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =[\\begin{aligned}{e}^{x+2y} & 2{e}^{x+2y}\\end{aligned}] \\\\ {f}^{′}(0,0) & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Calculating the second derivative, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{″}(x) & =[\\begin{aligned}{e}^{x+2y} & 2{e}^{x+2y} \\\\ 2{e}^{x+2y} & 4{e}^{x+2y}\\end{aligned}] \\\\ {f}^{″}(0,0) & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 4\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the second-degree Taylor polynomial is given by\n\n$$\nf(x)≈1+[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}]+\\frac{1}{2}{[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}]}^{T}[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 4\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nFinally, the sum of the missing expressions is\n\n$$\n1+2+x+x+2+x=3x+5.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186365647 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "e74c46c7-7f57-5adc-8acb-ea91958d8725" :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Quadratic Taylor Polynomial"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "dd867988-d331-5df8-a217-a6e6d2d4cf48" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186365649} {:answer/id #uuid "1bf2438e-0e53-5cd5-9d54-2682ae6286ce" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186365650} {:answer/id #uuid "d67fa04c-5935-541d-8571-73ebca3d2660" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186365651} {:answer/id #uuid "f34fb7f1-dc69-558f-abbc-8b4e1dff2ebb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186365652} {:answer/id #uuid "6700134e-b52b-56f8-b098-58eca236ad2b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186365653}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "1bf2438e-0e53-5cd5-9d54-2682ae6286ce" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186365650} :answer-field/id #uuid "8ddfd091-fe4f-54a2-b3ba-53ceedbdb333" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186365648}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "09e4111a-d204-5e67-864f-bf2698974078" :question/math-academy-id "q-163088" :question/problem "For the function of two variables \n$f(x,y)={e}^{xy},$\nthe quadratic Taylor polynomial of $f$ about $(0,0)$ is given by\n\n$$\nf(x,y)=a+bxy.\n$$\n\nFind the value of $a+b.$" :question/worked-solution "If the function $f(x,y)$ is twice differentiable at $(x,y)=(0,0),$ then the quadratic Taylor polynomial of $f$ about $(0,0)$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x,y) & ≈\\overset{\\text{Linear approximation}}{\\overset{⏞}{f(0,0)+{f}_{x}(0,0)\\cdot x+{f}_{y}(0,0)\\cdot y}} \\\\  & \\,\\,+{{\\frac{1}{2}({f}_{xx}(0,0)\\cdot {x}^{2}+2{f}_{xy}(0,0)\\cdot xy+{f}_{yy}(0,0)\\cdot {y}^{2})}_{⏟}}_{\\text{Quadratic part}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we compute the terms of our Taylor polynomial.\n\n- Calculating the value of the function, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(0,0) & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Calculating the first derivatives, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{x}(x,y) & =y{e}^{xy}, & \\,{f}_{y}(x,y) & =x{e}^{xy} \\\\ {f}_{x}(0,0) & =0, & \\,{f}_{y}(0,0) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Calculating the second derivatives, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{xx}(x,y) & ={y}^{2}{e}^{xy}, & \\,{f}_{xx}(0,0) & =0 \\\\ {f}_{xy}(x,y) & =(1+xy){e}^{xy}, & \\,{f}_{xy}(0,0) & =1 \\\\ {f}_{yy}(x,y) & ={x}^{2}{e}^{xy}, & \\,{f}_{yy}(0,0) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the second-degree Taylor polynomial is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x,y) & ≈1+0\\cdot x+0\\cdot y+\\frac{1}{2}(0\\cdot {x}^{2}+2(1)\\cdot xy+0\\cdot {y}^{2}) \\\\  & =1+1xy.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally,\n\n$$\na+b=1+1=2.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186365654 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "e74c46c7-7f57-5adc-8acb-ea91958d8725" :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Quadratic Taylor Polynomial"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "40811bb7-1293-567b-89ce-cbc69a5b7959" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186365656} {:answer/id #uuid "44d9555a-c4b2-58ee-8ccd-b522bf6ecee5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186365657} {:answer/id #uuid "e6ab957d-5bcf-5288-b5f4-9802b87bea76" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186365658} {:answer/id #uuid "cdde9978-b0b2-5d07-bc4d-ee13252b23b6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186365659} {:answer/id #uuid "75898e2e-61e5-5293-88a1-88d0764d0d04" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186365660}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "40811bb7-1293-567b-89ce-cbc69a5b7959" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186365656} :answer-field/id #uuid "cf83add5-1b5e-5d79-b4eb-3621f3990a1b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186365655}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "8475ec86-fc5b-5e5a-ae5d-7884b156aaaf" :question/math-academy-id "q-163089" :question/problem "For the function of two variables \n$f(x,y)=2{e}^{x-y},$\nthe quadratic Taylor polynomial of $f$ about $(0,0)$ is given by\n\n$$\nf(x,y)=2+ax-2y+b{x}^{2}+cxy+{y}^{2}.\n$$\n\nFind the value of $a+b+c.$" :question/worked-solution "If the function $f(x,y)$ is twice differentiable at $(x,y)=(0,0),$ then the quadratic Taylor polynomial of $f$ about $(0,0)$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x,y) & ≈\\overset{\\text{Linear approximation}}{\\overset{⏞}{f(0,0)+{f}_{x}(0,0)\\cdot x+{f}_{y}(0,0)\\cdot y}} \\\\  & \\,\\,+{{\\frac{1}{2}({f}_{xx}(0,0)\\cdot {x}^{2}+2{f}_{xy}(0,0)\\cdot xy+{f}_{yy}(0,0)\\cdot {y}^{2})}_{⏟}}_{\\text{Quadratic part}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we compute the terms of our Taylor polynomial.\n\n- Calculating the value of the function, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(0,0) & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Calculating the first derivatives, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{x}(x,y) & =2{e}^{x-y}, & \\,{f}_{y}(x,y) & =-2{e}^{x-y} \\\\ {f}_{x}(0,0) & =2, & \\,{f}_{y}(0,0) & =-2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Calculating the second derivatives, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{xx}(x,y) & =2{e}^{x-y}, & \\,{f}_{xx}(0,0) & =2 \\\\ {f}_{xy}(x,y) & =-2{e}^{x-y}, & \\,{f}_{xy}(0,0) & =-2 \\\\ {f}_{yy}(x,y) & =2{e}^{x-y}, & \\,{f}_{yy}(0,0) & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the second-degree Taylor polynomial is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x,y) & ≈2+2\\cdot x+(-2)\\cdot y+\\frac{1}{2}(2\\cdot {x}^{2}+2(-2)\\cdot xy+2\\cdot {y}^{2}) \\\\  & =2+2x-2y+1{x}^{2}+(-2)xy+{y}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally,\n\n$$\na+b=2+1+(-2)=1.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186365661 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "e74c46c7-7f57-5adc-8acb-ea91958d8725" :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Quadratic Taylor Polynomial"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "80d61f1d-21e2-5814-8552-97ce115148e5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186365663} {:answer/id #uuid "8b6a1cd5-3404-5292-903e-a02e1c231e41" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186365664} {:answer/id #uuid "3f500f9c-fab9-51b2-80c0-34fc1b4f5855" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186365665} {:answer/id #uuid "b0f359d0-adcd-599b-8d6f-e2c8df814eb1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186365666} {:answer/id #uuid "a0a99c7b-01f7-5d54-8513-7a7722c0dbb5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186365667}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a0a99c7b-01f7-5d54-8513-7a7722c0dbb5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186365667} :answer-field/id #uuid "85232b9d-9f60-55bd-8952-147c0422f663" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186365662}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "ab667448-646e-5808-a9d4-9ea4efc574ac" :question/math-academy-id "q-163090" :question/problem "For the function of two variables \n$f(x,y)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡({x}^{2}-2y),$\nthe quadratic Taylor polynomial of $f$ about $(0,0)$ is given by\n\n$$\nf(x,y)=ay+b{x}^{2}.\n$$\n\nFind the value of $a+b.$" :question/worked-solution "If the function $f(x,y)$ is twice differentiable at $(x,y)=(0,0),$ then the quadratic Taylor polynomial of $f$ about $(0,0)$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x,y) & ≈\\overset{\\text{Linear approximation}}{\\overset{⏞}{f(0,0)+{f}_{x}(0,0)\\cdot x+{f}_{y}(0,0)\\cdot y}} \\\\  & \\,\\,+{{\\frac{1}{2}({f}_{xx}(0,0)\\cdot {x}^{2}+2{f}_{xy}(0,0)\\cdot xy+{f}_{yy}(0,0)\\cdot {y}^{2})}_{⏟}}_{\\text{Quadratic part}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we compute the terms of our Taylor polynomial.\n\n- Calculating the value of the function, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(0,0) & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Calculating the first derivatives, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{x}(x,y) & =2x\\operatorname{cos}⁡({x}^{2}-2y), & \\,{f}_{y}(x,y) & =-2\\operatorname{cos}⁡({x}^{2}-2y) \\\\ {f}_{x}(0,0) & =0, & \\,{f}_{y}(0,0) & =-2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Calculating the second derivatives, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{xx}(x,y) & =2\\operatorname{cos}⁡({x}^{2}-2y)-4{x}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡({x}^{2}-2y), & \\,{f}_{xx}(0,0) & =2 \\\\ {f}_{xy}(x,y) & =4x\\operatorname{sin}⁡({x}^{2}-2y), & \\,{f}_{xy}(0,0) & =0 \\\\ {f}_{yy}(x,y) & =-4\\operatorname{sin}⁡({x}^{2}-2y), & \\,{f}_{yy}(0,0) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the second-degree Taylor polynomial is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x,y) & ≈0+0\\cdot x+(-2)\\cdot y+\\frac{1}{2}(2\\cdot {x}^{2}+2(0)\\cdot xy+0\\cdot {y}^{2}) \\\\  & =(-2)y+1{x}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally,\n\n$$\na+b=-2+1=-1.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186365668 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "e74c46c7-7f57-5adc-8acb-ea91958d8725" :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Quadratic Taylor Polynomial"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8d7b341a-0b47-5682-afb7-67ae9bcce5b4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186365670} {:answer/id #uuid "e04350a2-3924-5700-b400-3c458dcb999d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186365671} {:answer/id #uuid "b2d44128-9bb4-5c24-b614-ac9e433628e5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186365672} {:answer/id #uuid "54aaae59-6e1a-576a-a9cd-12e3efc654cc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186365673} {:answer/id #uuid "433fd0d1-ee00-5219-9466-7c353e8464dc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186365674}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "8d7b341a-0b47-5682-afb7-67ae9bcce5b4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186365670} :answer-field/id #uuid "ccbd110f-b29f-54c1-b938-928241b96a82" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186365669}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "08d7eda3-4cce-5e9b-945a-ceae353423e1" :question/math-academy-id "q-163095" :question/problem "For the function of two variables \n$f(x,y)={e}^{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}},$\nthe quadratic Taylor polynomial of $f$ about $(0,0)$ is given by\n\n$$\nf(x,y)=a+b{x}^{2}+c{y}^{2}.\n$$\n\nFind the value of $a+b+c.$" :question/worked-solution "If the function $f(x,y)$ is twice differentiable at $(x,y)=(0,0),$ then the quadratic Taylor polynomial of $f$ about $(0,0)$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x,y) & ≈\\overset{\\text{Linear approximation}}{\\overset{⏞}{f(0,0)+{f}_{x}(0,0)\\cdot x+{f}_{y}(0,0)\\cdot y}} \\\\  & \\,\\,+{{\\frac{1}{2}({f}_{xx}(0,0)\\cdot {x}^{2}+2{f}_{xy}(0,0)\\cdot xy+{f}_{yy}(0,0)\\cdot {y}^{2})}_{⏟}}_{\\text{Quadratic part}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we compute the terms of our Taylor polynomial.\n\n- Calculating the value of the function, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(0,0) & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Calculating the first derivatives, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{x}(x,y) & =2x{e}^{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}, & \\,{f}_{y}(x,y) & =2y{e}^{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}} \\\\ {f}_{x}(0,0) & =0, & \\,{f}_{y}(0,0) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Calculating the second derivatives, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{xx}(x,y) & =2(1+2{x}^{2}){e}^{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}, & \\,{f}_{xx}(0,0) & =2 \\\\ {f}_{xy}(x,y) & =4xy{e}^{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}, & \\,{f}_{xy}(0,0) & =0 \\\\ {f}_{yy}(x,y) & =2(1+2{y}^{2}){e}^{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}, & \\,{f}_{yy}(0,0) & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the second-degree Taylor polynomial is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x,y) & ≈1+0\\cdot x+0\\cdot y+\\frac{1}{2}(2\\cdot {x}^{2}+2(0)\\cdot xy+2\\cdot {y}^{2}) \\\\  & =1+1{x}^{2}+1{y}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally,\n\n$$\na+b+c=1+1+1=3.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198582 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "e74c46c7-7f57-5adc-8acb-ea91958d8725" :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Quadratic Taylor Polynomial"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "ffc96c81-5b7c-58cd-9b5d-6dfd9bb0b790" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186194394} {:answer/id #uuid "3ee0489b-8a57-51af-bea6-34e805385b8a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186194395} {:answer/id #uuid "489f087f-0bcd-515e-9cfd-1985473f4899" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186194396} {:answer/id #uuid "5dc3c602-1423-5a20-94a7-1b05712f4b01" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186194397} {:answer/id #uuid "757dad5f-5217-5ec6-994f-193ccd359d89" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186194398}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "3ee0489b-8a57-51af-bea6-34e805385b8a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186194395} :answer-field/id #uuid "de5213ac-c95b-516a-8ebe-fbfb3ef5e679" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186194393}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "6e6d1ba4-7501-5c84-a8cd-0bbe2d40c544" :question/math-academy-id "q-163113" :question/problem "For the function of two variables \n$f(x,y)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x-2y),$\nthe quadratic Taylor polynomial of $f$ about $(0,0)$ is given by\n\n$$\nf(x,y)=1+a{x}^{2}+bxy+c{y}^{2}.\n$$\n\nFind the value of $a+b+c.$" :question/worked-solution "If the function $f(x,y)$ is twice differentiable at $(x,y)=(0,0),$ then the quadratic Taylor polynomial of $f$ about $(0,0)$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x,y) & ≈\\overset{\\text{Linear approximation}}{\\overset{⏞}{f(0,0)+{f}_{x}(0,0)\\cdot x+{f}_{y}(0,0)\\cdot y}} \\\\  & \\,\\,+{{\\frac{1}{2}({f}_{xx}(0,0)\\cdot {x}^{2}+2{f}_{xy}(0,0)\\cdot xy+{f}_{yy}(0,0)\\cdot {y}^{2})}_{⏟}}_{\\text{Quadratic part}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we compute the terms of our Taylor polynomial.\n\n- Calculating the value of the function, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(0,0) & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Calculating the first derivatives, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{x}(x,y) & =-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x-2y), & \\,{f}_{y}(x,y) & =2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x-2y) \\\\ {f}_{x}(0,0) & =0, & \\,{f}_{y}(0,0) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Calculating the second derivatives, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{xx}(x,y) & =-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x-2y), & \\,{f}_{xx}(0,0) & =-1 \\\\ {f}_{xy}(x,y) & =2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x-2y), & \\,{f}_{xy}(0,0) & =2 \\\\ {f}_{yy}(x,y) & =-4\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x-2y), & \\,{f}_{yy}(0,0) & =-4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the second-degree Taylor polynomial is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x,y) & ≈1+0\\cdot x+0\\cdot y+\\frac{1}{2}(-1\\cdot {x}^{2}+2(2)\\cdot xy+(-4)\\cdot {y}^{2}) \\\\  & =1+(-\\frac{1}{2}){x}^{2}+2xy+(-2){y}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally,\n\n$$\na+b+c=(-\\frac{1}{2})+2+(-2)=-\\frac{1}{2}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186365675 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "b91ae708-a63d-51ac-9ae0-f24724392858" :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Quadratic Taylor Polynomial in Matrix Form"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a7c39ee6-f23c-50c9-97df-fd8db40b12fa" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2x-\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186365677} {:answer/id #uuid "2e233069-0620-531e-a4a1-9ad51fc8db54" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2x-\\frac{3\\pi }{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186365678} {:answer/id #uuid "043c221b-9897-5880-ace6-5b675d2c4483" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x-2\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186365679} {:answer/id #uuid "0dd1da3c-e40f-5f05-b716-ed26a9e94647" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x-\\frac{\\pi }{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186365680} {:answer/id #uuid "a9308028-908f-59f3-822e-aa779133bd5a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186365681}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "2e233069-0620-531e-a4a1-9ad51fc8db54" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2x-\\frac{3\\pi }{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186365678} :answer-field/id #uuid "fcbfbb90-43e5-5bc1-ad8c-f4050b524791" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186365676}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "0190bbcd-1adb-5d8e-b626-c2bdc5965081" :question/math-academy-id "q-163122" :question/problem "For the function $f:{R}^{2}\\to R,$ defined as\n\n$$\nf(x,y)=x\\operatorname{cos}⁡y+y\\operatorname{sin}⁡x,\n$$\n\nthe quadratic Taylor polynomial of $f$ about $a=[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\pi }{2} \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]$ is given by\n\n$$\nf(x,y)≈f(a)+{f}^{′}(a)(x-a)+\\frac{1}{2}{[\\begin{aligned}∗ \\\\ y\\end{aligned}]}^{T}[\\begin{aligned}0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & ∗\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}∗ \\\\ y\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nFind the sum of the three missing expressions." :question/worked-solution "Suppose that the function $f:{R}^{n}\\to R$ is defined by \n\n$$\nf:[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2} \\\\ ⋮ \\\\ {x}_{n}\\end{aligned}]⟶z\n$$\n\n \n\nand is twice differentiable at $x=a.$ Then, the quadratic Taylor polynomial of $f$ about $a$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & ≈f(a)+{f}^{′}(a)(x-a)+\\frac{1}{2}(x-a)^{T}{f}^{″}(a)(x-a),\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(a)={[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂{x}_{1}} & \\frac{∂f}{∂{x}_{2}} & ⋯ & \\frac{∂f}{∂{x}_{n}}\\end{aligned}]|}_{x=a}\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n{f}^{″}(a)={[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{1}^{2}} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{1}∂{x}_{2}} & ⋯ & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{1}∂{x}_{n}} \\\\ \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{2}∂{x}_{1}} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{2}^{2}} & ⋯ & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{2}∂{x}_{n}} \\\\ ⋮ & ⋮ & ⋮ & ⋮ \\\\ \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{n}∂{x}_{1}} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{n}∂{x}_{2}} & ⋯ & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{n}^{2}}\\end{aligned}]}_{x=a}\n$$\n\nIn this case, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x} & \\frac{∂f}{∂y}\\end{aligned}], & \\,{f}^{″}(x) & =[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂x∂y} \\\\ \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂y∂x} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{y}^{2}}\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nCalculating the first derivative, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =[\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡y+y\\operatorname{cos}⁡x & \\operatorname{sin}⁡x-x\\operatorname{sin}⁡y\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nCalculating the second derivative, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{″}(x) & =[\\begin{aligned}-y\\operatorname{sin}⁡x & \\operatorname{cos}⁡x-\\operatorname{sin}⁡y \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}⁡x-\\operatorname{sin}⁡y & -x\\operatorname{cos}⁡y\\end{aligned}] \\\\ {f}^{″}(\\frac{\\pi }{2},0) & =[\\begin{aligned}0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the second-degree Taylor polynomial is given by\n\n$$\nf(x)≈f(a)+{f}^{′}(a)(x-a)+\\frac{1}{2}{[\\begin{aligned}x-\\frac{\\pi }{2} \\\\ y\\end{aligned}]}^{T}[\\begin{aligned}0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}x-\\frac{\\pi }{2} \\\\ y\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nFinally, the sum of the missing expressions is\n\n$$\n(x-\\frac{\\pi }{2})+(-\\frac{\\pi }{2})+(x-\\frac{\\pi }{2})=2x-\\frac{3\\pi }{2}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186365682 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "b91ae708-a63d-51ac-9ae0-f24724392858" :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Quadratic Taylor Polynomial in Matrix Form"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "7f794083-d060-5d0a-a692-fd79c5c5ebda" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3x+1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186365684} {:answer/id #uuid "53f86935-8c5d-5aa3-97d4-f430b1f87e65" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2x-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186365685} {:answer/id #uuid "04d7da69-5a94-5ae4-be78-2ac0cf3c50d9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3x-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186365686} {:answer/id #uuid "57b97a26-4546-5958-b61d-e6e29e2ee2f5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3x+4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186365687} {:answer/id #uuid "032e3033-1ec8-50ce-9507-c9fd6fe77289" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186365688}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "04d7da69-5a94-5ae4-be78-2ac0cf3c50d9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3x-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186365686} :answer-field/id #uuid "3ffe02ef-0fa4-520c-bcde-7e0a4d0f036f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186365683}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "0904ce8e-c75e-58cc-88f6-cd03e0208378" :question/math-academy-id "q-163142" :question/problem "For the function $f:{R}^{2}\\to R,$ defined as\n\n$$\nf(x,y)=x\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x-y),\n$$\n\nthe quadratic Taylor polynomial of $f$ about $a=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]$ is given by\n\n$$\nf(x)≈∗+[\\begin{aligned}1 & ∗\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}∗ \\\\ y-1\\end{aligned}]+\\frac{1}{2}{[\\begin{aligned}∗ \\\\ y-1\\end{aligned}]}^{T}[\\begin{aligned}∗ & -1 \\\\ -1 & 0\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}∗ \\\\ y-1\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nFind the sum of the six missing expressions." :question/worked-solution "Suppose that the function $f:{R}^{n}\\to R$ is defened by \n\n$$\nf:[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2} \\\\ ⋮ \\\\ {x}_{n}\\end{aligned}]⟶z\n$$\n\n \n\nand is twice differentiable at $x=a.$ Then, the quadratic Taylor polynomial of $f$ about $a$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & ≈f(a)+{f}^{′}(a)(x-a)+\\frac{1}{2}(x-a)^{T}{f}^{″}(a)(x-a),\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(a)={[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂{x}_{1}} & \\frac{∂f}{∂{x}_{2}} & ⋯ & \\frac{∂f}{∂{x}_{n}}\\end{aligned}]|}_{x=a}\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n{f}^{″}(a)={[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{1}^{2}} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{1}∂{x}_{2}} & ⋯ & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{1}∂{x}_{n}} \\\\ \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{2}∂{x}_{1}} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{2}^{2}} & ⋯ & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{2}∂{x}_{n}} \\\\ ⋮ & ⋮ & ⋮ & ⋮ \\\\ \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{n}∂{x}_{1}} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{n}∂{x}_{2}} & ⋯ & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{n}^{2}}\\end{aligned}]}_{x=a}\n$$\n\nIn this case, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x} & \\frac{∂f}{∂y}\\end{aligned}], & \\,{f}^{″}(x) & =[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂x∂y} \\\\ \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂y∂x} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{y}^{2}}\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we compute the terms of our Taylor polynomial:\n\n- Calculating the value of the function, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(1,1) & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Calculating the first derivatives, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =[\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x-y)+x\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x-y) & -x\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x-y)\\end{aligned}] \\\\ {f}^{′}(1,1) & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Calculating the second derivative, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{″}(x) & =[\\begin{aligned}2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x-y)-x\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x-y) & x\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x-y)-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x-y) \\\\ x\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x-y)-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x-y) & -x\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x-y)\\end{aligned}] \\\\ {f}^{″}(1,1) & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & -1 \\\\ -1 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the second-degree Taylor polynomial is given by\n\n$$\nf(x)≈0+[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}x-1 \\\\ y-1\\end{aligned}]+\\frac{1}{2}{[\\begin{aligned}x-1 \\\\ y-1\\end{aligned}]}^{T}[\\begin{aligned}2 & -1 \\\\ -1 & 0\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}x-1 \\\\ y-1\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nFinally, the sum of the missing expressions is\n\n$$\n0+(-1)+(x-1)+(x-1)+2+(x-1)=3x-2.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186365689 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "b91ae708-a63d-51ac-9ae0-f24724392858" :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Quadratic Taylor Polynomial in Matrix Form"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a6c95159-ce26-5e7b-89ab-5b7168cd887a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3y-3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186365691} {:answer/id #uuid "ef95f9dd-5c85-5892-af4d-435a3c43fcd6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3y+10" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186365692} {:answer/id #uuid "5e9b6389-e15d-5085-86ce-5cfabf82a24c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3y+7" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186365693} {:answer/id #uuid "18e7f712-e84a-5f88-9a67-9268cc5fba01" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2y-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186365694} {:answer/id #uuid "22d44696-3b15-591e-a705-b93af0949f6a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2y+8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186365695}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "5e9b6389-e15d-5085-86ce-5cfabf82a24c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3y+7" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186365693} :answer-field/id #uuid "c2764e4c-6fa2-56ea-9f98-4d8170dc89f6" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186365690}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "d3e01033-1d2b-5a46-9005-4a8dee4e592c" :question/math-academy-id "q-163143" :question/problem "For the function $f:{R}^{2}\\to R,$ defined as\n\n$$\nf(x,y)=\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+y)+{x}^{2}+{y}^{3},\n$$\n\nthe quadratic Taylor polynomial of $f$ about $a=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]$ is given by\n\n$$\nf(x)≈∗+[\\begin{aligned}1 & ∗\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ ∗\\end{aligned}]+\\frac{1}{2}{[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ ∗\\end{aligned}]}^{T}[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 \\\\ -1 & ∗\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ ∗\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nFind the sum of the six missing expressions." :question/worked-solution "Suppose that the function $f:{R}^{n}\\to R$ is defened by \n\n$$\nf:[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2} \\\\ ⋮ \\\\ {x}_{n}\\end{aligned}]⟶z\n$$\n\n \n\nand is twice differentiable at $x=a.$ Then, the quadratic Taylor polynomial of $f$ about $a$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & ≈f(a)+{f}^{′}(a)(x-a)+\\frac{1}{2}(x-a)^{T}{f}^{″}(a)(x-a),\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(a)={[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂{x}_{1}} & \\frac{∂f}{∂{x}_{2}} & ⋯ & \\frac{∂f}{∂{x}_{n}}\\end{aligned}]|}_{x=a}\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n{f}^{″}(a)={[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{1}^{2}} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{1}∂{x}_{2}} & ⋯ & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{1}∂{x}_{n}} \\\\ \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{2}∂{x}_{1}} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{2}^{2}} & ⋯ & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{2}∂{x}_{n}} \\\\ ⋮ & ⋮ & ⋮ & ⋮ \\\\ \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{n}∂{x}_{1}} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{n}∂{x}_{2}} & ⋯ & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{n}^{2}}\\end{aligned}]}_{x=a}\n$$\n\nIn this case, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x} & \\frac{∂f}{∂y}\\end{aligned}], & \\,{f}^{″}(x) & =[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂x∂y} \\\\ \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂y∂x} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{y}^{2}}\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we compute the terms of our Taylor polynomial:\n\n- Calculating the value of the function, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(0,1) & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Calculating the first derivatives, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{x+y}+2x & \\frac{1}{x+y}+3{y}^{2}\\end{aligned}] \\\\ {f}^{′}(0,1) & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 4\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Calculating the second derivative, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{″}(x) & =[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{1}{(x+y)^{2}}+2 & -\\frac{1}{(x+y)^{2}} \\\\ -\\frac{1}{(x+y)^{2}} & -\\frac{1}{(x+y)^{2}}+6y\\end{aligned}] \\\\ {f}^{″}(0,1) & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 \\\\ -1 & 5\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the second-degree Taylor polynomial is given by\n\n$$\nf(x)≈1+[\\begin{aligned}1 & 4\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y-1\\end{aligned}]+\\frac{1}{2}{[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y-1\\end{aligned}]}^{T}[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 \\\\ -1 & 5\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y-1\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nFinally, the sum of the missing expressions is\n\n$$\n1+4+(y-1)+(y-1)+5+(y-1)=3y+7.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186365696 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "b91ae708-a63d-51ac-9ae0-f24724392858" :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Quadratic Taylor Polynomial in Matrix Form"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "d8bce873-d0f1-51e4-a459-46a3564149d7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x+y+10e" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186365698} {:answer/id #uuid "0da4d623-b363-5688-a538-c4e4b4efede2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2y+6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186365699} {:answer/id #uuid "0127290b-b334-513e-a8de-215a0f060358" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x+y-3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186365700} {:answer/id #uuid "eda7c3dd-3df5-5f68-9574-db5bc7138647" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2y+10e-4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186365701} {:answer/id #uuid "1b4936b9-093f-5a86-bcb5-ee787894daf6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2y+6e-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186365702}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "eda7c3dd-3df5-5f68-9574-db5bc7138647" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2y+10e-4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186365701} :answer-field/id #uuid "37fee235-295d-504a-8074-cc3c625a58f2" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186365697}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "dec6487d-cd5d-5846-9c3b-14210b1524e6" :question/math-academy-id "q-163144" :question/problem "For the function $f:{R}^{2}\\to R,$ defined as\n\n$$\nf(x,y)={e}^{3x-y},\n$$\n\nthe quadratic Taylor polynomial of $f$ about $a=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]$ is given by\n\n$$\nf(x)≈∗+[\\begin{aligned}∗ & -e\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}x-1 \\\\ ∗\\end{aligned}]+\\frac{1}{2}{[\\begin{aligned}x-1 \\\\ ∗\\end{aligned}]}^{T}[\\begin{aligned}∗ & ∗ \\\\ -3e & e\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}x-1 \\\\ y-2\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nFind the sum of the six missing expressions." :question/worked-solution "Suppose that the function $f:{R}^{n}\\to R$ is defened by \n\n$$\nf:[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2} \\\\ ⋮ \\\\ {x}_{n}\\end{aligned}]⟶z\n$$\n\n \n\nand is twice differentiable at $x=a.$ Then, the quadratic Taylor polynomial of $f$ about $a$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & ≈f(a)+{f}^{′}(a)(x-a)+\\frac{1}{2}(x-a)^{T}{f}^{″}(a)(x-a),\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(a)={[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂{x}_{1}} & \\frac{∂f}{∂{x}_{2}} & ⋯ & \\frac{∂f}{∂{x}_{n}}\\end{aligned}]|}_{x=a}\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n{f}^{″}(a)={[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{1}^{2}} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{1}∂{x}_{2}} & ⋯ & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{1}∂{x}_{n}} \\\\ \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{2}∂{x}_{1}} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{2}^{2}} & ⋯ & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{2}∂{x}_{n}} \\\\ ⋮ & ⋮ & ⋮ & ⋮ \\\\ \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{n}∂{x}_{1}} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{n}∂{x}_{2}} & ⋯ & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{n}^{2}}\\end{aligned}]}_{x=a}\n$$\n\nIn this case, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x} & \\frac{∂f}{∂y}\\end{aligned}], & \\,{f}^{″}(x) & =[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂x∂y} \\\\ \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂y∂x} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{y}^{2}}\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we compute the terms of our Taylor polynomial:\n\n- Calculating the value of the function, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(1,2) & =e.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Calculating the first derivatives, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =[\\begin{aligned}3{e}^{3x-y} & -{e}^{3x-y}\\end{aligned}] \\\\ {f}^{′}(1,2) & =[\\begin{aligned}3e & -e\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Calculating the second derivative, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{″}(x) & =[\\begin{aligned}9{e}^{3x-y} & -3{e}^{3x-y} \\\\ -3{e}^{3x-y} & {e}^{3x-y}\\end{aligned}] \\\\ {f}^{″}(1,2) & =[\\begin{aligned}9e & -3e \\\\ -3e & e\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the second-degree Taylor polynomial is given by\n\n$$\nf(x)≈e+[\\begin{aligned}3e & -e\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}x-1 \\\\ y-2\\end{aligned}]+\\frac{1}{2}{[\\begin{aligned}x-1 \\\\ y-2\\end{aligned}]}^{T}[\\begin{aligned}9e & -3e \\\\ -3e & e\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}x-1 \\\\ y-2\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nFinally, the sum of the missing expressions is\n\n$$\ne+3e+(y-2)+(y-2)+9e+(-3e)=2y+10e-4.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186365703 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "b19cc766-bdc5-5ab2-aa4d-ebddf8311116" :knowledge-point/title "Approximating a Multivariable Function at a Point Using a Quadratic Taylor Polynomial"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b07906f3-600e-54a2-bada-65c7b3188e0f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186365705} {:answer/id #uuid "37bd7c75-dbdd-59a5-90d4-2bcc1a06c6d0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186365706} {:answer/id #uuid "26ceed56-a138-5d73-94c1-b3aa44f2ab94" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186365707} {:answer/id #uuid "4a1cb40e-fe0e-5c8f-b786-67cfb3194d9a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186365708} {:answer/id #uuid "33b74a57-1060-597c-861b-6cbca