⋮ \\\\ {x}_{n}\\end{aligned}]⟶z\n$$\n\n \n\nand is twice differentiable at $x=a.$ Then, the quadratic Taylor polynomial of $f$ about $a$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & ≈f(a)+{f}^{′}(a)(x-a)+\\frac{1}{2}(x-a)^{T}{f}^{″}(a)(x-a),\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(a)={[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂{x}_{1}} & \\frac{∂f}{∂{x}_{2}} & ⋯ & \\frac{∂f}{∂{x}_{n}}\\end{aligned}]|}_{x=a}\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n{f}^{″}(a)={[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{1}^{2}} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{1}∂{x}_{2}} & ⋯ & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{1}∂{x}_{n}} \\\\ \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{2}∂{x}_{1}} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{2}^{2}} & ⋯ & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{2}∂{x}_{n}} \\\\ ⋮ & ⋮ & ⋮ & ⋮ \\\\ \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{n}∂{x}_{1}} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{n}∂{x}_{2}} & ⋯ & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{n}^{2}}\\end{aligned}]}_{x=a}\n$$\n\nIn this case, we have $a=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}],$ and the following data:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(a) & =5, & \\,{f}^{′}(a) & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2\\end{aligned}], & \\,{f}^{″}(a) & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & 4 \\\\ 4 & 1\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the second-degree Taylor polynomial is given by\n\n$$\nf(x)≈5+[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}]+\\frac{1}{2}{[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}]}^{T}[\\begin{aligned}2 & 4 \\\\ 4 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nFinally, evaluating the polynomial at $x=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}],$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(2,2) & ≈5+[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]+\\frac{1}{2}{[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]}^{T}[\\begin{aligned}2 & 4 \\\\ 4 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =5+[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]+\\frac{1}{2}{[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]}^{T}[\\begin{aligned}2 & 4 \\\\ 4 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =5+6+\\frac{1}{2}[\\begin{aligned}2 & 2\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}12 \\\\ 10\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =11+\\frac{1}{2}(44) \\\\  & =33.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186365724 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "b19cc766-bdc5-5ab2-aa4d-ebddf8311116" :knowledge-point/title "Approximating a Multivariable Function at a Point Using a Quadratic Taylor Polynomial"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "f5156b65-1743-5a69-8c40-b8707d868628" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186365726} {:answer/id #uuid "6fdca671-e418-5a1c-8e63-21b19eb9c977" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186365727} {:answer/id #uuid "0ee971a7-50b6-5956-90c9-5c866af4e54d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186365728} {:answer/id #uuid "ce2376dd-01bc-561e-973d-63cfd2c8bf13" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186365729} {:answer/id #uuid "910c22ab-950e-53b9-b96e-dd70e5a793ca" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186365730}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "6fdca671-e418-5a1c-8e63-21b19eb9c977" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186365727} :answer-field/id #uuid "9347efe4-c4ef-529d-aa59-92df187b1c1b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186365725}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "bdec46be-da01-5e43-9611-edf57f3a506b" :question/math-academy-id "q-164858" :question/problem "For the twice-differentiable function $f:{R}^{3}\\to R,$ we have the following data:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(0,1,1) & =1, & \\,{f}^{′}(0,1,1) & =[\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 & -1\\end{aligned}], & \\,{f}^{″}(0,1,1) & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -2\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUse the quadratic Taylor polynomial of $f$ about $a=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]$ to approximate the value of $f(1,2,1).$" :question/worked-solution "Suppose that the function $f:{R}^{n}\\to R$ is defined by \n\n$$\nf:[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2} \\\\ ⋮ \\\\ {x}_{n}\\end{aligned}]⟶z\n$$\n\n \n\nand is twice differentiable at $x=a.$ Then, the quadratic Taylor polynomial of $f$ about $a$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & ≈f(a)+{f}^{′}(a)(x-a)+\\frac{1}{2}(x-a)^{T}{f}^{″}(a)(x-a),\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(a)={[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂{x}_{1}} & \\frac{∂f}{∂{x}_{2}} & ⋯ & \\frac{∂f}{∂{x}_{n}}\\end{aligned}]|}_{x=a}\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n{f}^{″}(a)={[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{1}^{2}} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{1}∂{x}_{2}} & ⋯ & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{1}∂{x}_{n}} \\\\ \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{2}∂{x}_{1}} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{2}^{2}} & ⋯ & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{2}∂{x}_{n}} \\\\ ⋮ & ⋮ & ⋮ & ⋮ \\\\ \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{n}∂{x}_{1}} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{n}∂{x}_{2}} & ⋯ & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{n}^{2}}\\end{aligned}]}_{x=a}\n$$\n\nIn this case, we have $a=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}],$ and the following data:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(a) & =1, & \\,{f}^{′}(a) & =[\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 & -1\\end{aligned}], & \\,{f}^{″}(a) & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -2\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the second-degree Taylor polynomial is given by\n\n$$\nf(x)≈1+[\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 & -1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y-1 \\\\ z-1\\end{aligned}]+\\frac{1}{2}{[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y-1 \\\\ z-1\\end{aligned}]}^{T}[\\begin{aligned}2 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -2\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y-1 \\\\ z-1\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nFinally, evaluating the polynomial at $x=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}],$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(1,2,1) & ≈1+[\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 & -1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 2-1 \\\\ 1-1\\end{aligned}]+\\frac{1}{2}{[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 2-1 \\\\ 1-1\\end{aligned}]}^{T}[\\begin{aligned}2 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -2\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 2-1 \\\\ 1-1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =1+[\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 & -1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]+\\frac{1}{2}{[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]}^{T}[\\begin{aligned}2 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -2\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =1+1+\\frac{1}{2}[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 & 0\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =2+\\frac{1}{2}(2) \\\\  & =3.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186365731 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "b19cc766-bdc5-5ab2-aa4d-ebddf8311116" :knowledge-point/title "Approximating a Multivariable Function at a Point Using a Quadratic Taylor Polynomial"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "226e8e56-365a-58eb-97bc-bd3fdd2ddcd1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-11" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186365733} {:answer/id #uuid "84c4a917-a13c-54c6-820f-b8e516401273" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186365734} {:answer/id #uuid "424738d3-4009-551f-9aab-d2c1098d8510" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186365735} {:answer/id #uuid "a3751ab5-cbfb-5b1a-93b3-930dab3cdb02" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186365736} {:answer/id #uuid "fa468c32-57c6-5e31-81bf-741d3863bb90" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186365737}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "84c4a917-a13c-54c6-820f-b8e516401273" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186365734} :answer-field/id #uui